# Beat 12 of grpconf/synopsis.md — 1:00, the epilogue.
#
# Payload:  grpconf/stage/boblog, 20 243 bytes — the same file again.
# Database: grpconf/stage/googleapis.desc — 25 660 332 B, 7 771 files,
#           58 777 types.  Not made for this talk, and the better for it.
#
# Invocation:
#   protolens --descriptor-set $GOOGLEAPIS -I $SRC2 \
#       --script grpconf/beats/scale.script $BOBLOG
#
# THIS BEAT IS DROPPABLE.  If beat 10 overran, cut it and say the
# closing quote from the podium instead.  It sets no overrides and
# reads nothing forward, so nothing downstream depends on it.
#
# It answers the scale question before the Q&A does, and gets a second
# thing for the same minute: the third point that turns two data points
# from a slogan into a curve.
#
# No overrides.  Three steps.
#
# Re-pin (must print nothing):
#   protolens --descriptor-set $GOOGLEAPIS -I $SRC2 \
#       --script grpconf/beats/scale.script $BOBLOG script \
#     | grep 'error:'

title: Two hundred times the dictionary

steps:
  - text: |
      Somebody is going to ask whether this works against a real
      corpus.  Answer it now.

      7 771 files.  58 777 types.  25.6 MB, indexed once.  The same
      20 KB log just opened against it in 82 ms — against 17 ms for
      the 130 kB database we were using a minute ago.

      Two hundred times the dictionary costs five times the wait, and
      the wait is a tenth of a second.  Startup scales with the
      payload, not with the descriptor set.

      Press space, then -> to step.
    fold: all
    unfold: [/]
    node: /

  - text: |
      And now the half nobody expects.

      The envelope went dark.  The best googleapis can offer for this
      root is a crowd of candidates tied at -2, because Google has
      never heard of Bob's app — so there is no bobapp.v1.Log here, no
      entry, no method, no request.

      The shapes are all still there.  Every name is gone.
    fold: all
    unfold: [/]
    node: /1

  - text: |
      The key is still in the file, and the tool still walks down to
      it — /1/4/1/3/2/3/1 — because the wire says how to.  It just
      cannot tell you that any of those numbers is called anything.

      Two hundred times the dictionary, and it knows less about this
      file than the one I built out of Bob's Downloads folder.

      More schema is not more knowledge.  The right schema is.

      Leave the session loaded.  Questions get answered on the tool.
    fold: all
    unfold: [/1]
    node: x-goog-api-key
